description:
Encode and decode names in DNS messages (lesson 1.03), including compression pointers, and build a query:

```cpp
std::optional<std::vector<std::uint8_t>> encodeName(const std::string& name);
std::optional<std::string> decodeName(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& packet, std::size_t& at);
std::vector<std::uint8_t> buildQuery(std::uint16_t id, const std::string& name, std::uint16_t type);
```

- **encodeName**: each label preceded by its length, then a zero byte. A trailing dot is allowed (`example.com.`); the root name `.` or `""` encodes as a single zero. Empty labels (`a..b`), labels longer than 63 bytes, or an encoded name longer than 255 bytes give `std::nullopt`.
- **decodeName** reads a name starting at `packet[at]`, returning it with dots between labels (the root as `"."`), and moves `at` past the name **as stored at that position** (for a name ending in a pointer, just past the 2-byte pointer). A byte with the top two bits set (`0xC0`) starts a 2-byte pointer: the low 14 bits are an offset in the packet where the name continues. Fail on running past the end, label lengths 64 to 191 (top bits `01` or `10`), or more than 20 pointers (a loop).
- **buildQuery**: a 12-byte header (id, flags `0x0100` meaning "recursion desired", one question, no other records, all 16-bit big-endian), the encoded name, the type, and class 1. An unencodable name gives an empty vector.

